    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_79499fc906469364ae4de568aa4ae847dc3dbe0cddcef340758241ab07a9e0d5",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The asymptotic argument first converts smoothing into a regime-sensitive localization device rather than claiming uniform suppression everywhere. The sharpened Mellin estimate retains exponential decay in contour height; for integral k the resulting kernel bound has two regimes, A^k for k<=A and arbitrary-power decay in kA only for k>A, while for real r a contour shift plus Stirling gives arbitrary-power tail decay once |r| is at least of order T(log T)^2 under the stated A window. These are kernel estimates that prepare, but do not themselves complete, suppression after separate spectral-weight bounds are supplied.",
